# Build context exclusions for the agent image (AGENTS.md §2, ARCH §8).
#
# The context is the repository root because agent/Dockerfile COPYs repo-relative
# paths. Without this file the daemon uploads everything, including host build
# artefacts that would otherwise be baked into a production image.
#
# __pycache__ matters specifically: it holds bytecode compiled by whatever
# interpreter the developer happened to use. Copying it into an image built on a
# different Python version ships stale, useless bytecode and inflates the layer.

# Host build artefacts
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/*.egg-info/
**/venv/
**/.venv/

# Version control
.git/
.github/

# Credentials. .gitignore already keeps these out of git, which is a different
# channel entirely: the build context is uploaded to the Docker daemon on every
# `docker build -f agent/Dockerfile .`, and ci.yaml L212 does exactly that. A file
# that .gitignore protects is still shipped in the context unless this list says
# otherwise, so the two have to be kept in step deliberately rather than assumed.
#
# No COPY in agent/Dockerfile reaches the repository root - the sources are
# ${PYTHON_DEPS} and agent/ alone - so none of this was ever baked into a layer.
# The exposure was the upload itself, which matters more the moment a build runs
# against a remote or cloud builder instead of the local daemon.
opencode.json
.mcp.json
.env*
!.env.example
!.env.sample
!.env.template
kubeconfig
kubeconfig.yaml
*.kubeconfig
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519
.aws/
.gcloud/
credentials.json
service-account*.json
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc
secrets.yaml
secrets.yml

# Test fixtures are not needed at runtime
tests/

# Nothing outside agent/ is consumed by the image
internal/
cmd/
docs/
*.md
